# `cp .env.example .env` gives `docker compose` its interpolation values. Nothing on the
# `make deps-up` path reads the copy — not the Makefile, not scripts/db_init.py, not any
# Python at import — so `make deps-up` straight after the copy fails with `FATAL:
# PROXYSHOP_WORKER is unset`. Load it into the shell first — this is step 1 of
# docs/deploy.md and of docs/demo/starting-slice.md, and it is one line:
#
#     set -a && . ./.env && set +a
#
# ONE OTHER THING READS `.env`, and it is worth knowing before you edit the port block:
# scripts/verify.sh sources it with `set -a` on every `make verify`, exporting every
# assignment here into the test process and shielding only PROXYSHOP_WORKER. A `.env`
# carrying relocated ports therefore re-points the whole suite at a stack that may not be
# running — measured at 133 skips and 2 failures against 0 skips with no `.env` present.
# An earlier revision of this header claimed "no script sources it"; that was wrong, and it
# was wrong because it was reasoned from a grep for `dotenv` across *.py when the loader is
# shell. docs/deploy.md carries the measurement.
#
# Worker index (D38). Every worker owns its own Postgres database (proxyshop_w<N>), its own
# Redis logical DB and its own `w<N>:` key prefix, so two things never share state.
#
# NOT 0. Worker 0 is RESERVED for this project's scored measurement run; a demo or a
# deployment that takes it corrupts the number the project is graded on. 1 is the default
# here and is what both runbooks now tell an operator to use. (An earlier revision of
# docs/demo/starting-slice.md said `export PROXYSHOP_WORKER=0` and disagreed with this line;
# the runbook was the side that was wrong.)
PROXYSHOP_WORKER=1

# The four least-privilege roles' password has ONE source of truth (T-112). It is seeded by
# db/init/00-roles.sql on a fresh volume and by db/migrations/0001 in every worker database,
# forwarded into the postgres container by docker-compose.yml, and read back on the connect
# side by proxyshop_support.postgres.role_dsn — all from this one variable. Leave it UNSET to
# get the documented dev default, whose single copy lives in 00-roles.sql's coalesce();
# setting it here (or in the shell before `make deps-up`) changes the seed and the connect
# side together. It must be set BEFORE the pgdata volume is created: the initdb hook runs
# once.
#
# LEAVING IT UNSET NOW ACTUALLY WORKS IN THE CONTAINERS TOO, which it did not before. The
# service fragments used to interpolate `${PROXYSHOP_ROLE_PASSWORD:-}` into their DSNs, so an
# unset variable produced `postgresql://exchange:@postgres:5432/...` — an EXPLICITLY EMPTY
# password, which libpq rejects before it reaches the server (`fe_sendauth: no password
# supplied`). docs/deploy.md therefore told operators the variable was REQUIRED while this
# file told them to leave it unset, and `cp .env.example .env` produced the configuration
# that could not serve. The fragments now carry password-free DSNs and resolve the credential
# at container start through role_dsn, the same function the T-112 tests pin, so the two
# files agree and the default path is the working one.
# PROXYSHOP_ROLE_PASSWORD=

# Postgres (D38). The DATABASE component below is ADVISORY: proxyshop_support.postgres
# rewrites it to proxyshop_w$PROXYSHOP_WORKER on every connection, so a .env copied between
# worktrees can never put two workers in one database. Host and port are taken from these
# values; all five are optional (the compose defaults are identical). NONE of the five
# carries a password on purpose (T-112) — a literal in a file every worktree copies is a
# copy that stops agreeing with the cluster the moment the credential changes. The four
# role passwords come from PROXYSHOP_ROLE_PASSWORD above; admin's comes from
# proxyshop_support.postgres.ROLES, which a test pins to docker-compose.yml's
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD. Add a password back here only to override one role, and know that
# PROXYSHOP_ROLE_PASSWORD outranks it.
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_ADMIN=postgresql://proxyshop@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_EXCHANGE=postgresql://exchange@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_TRUST_RW=postgresql://trust_rw@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_VAULT=postgresql://buyer_vault@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
PROXYSHOP_PG_DSN_APP=postgresql://app@localhost:5432/proxyshop_w1
NEO4J_URI=bolt://localhost:7687
NEO4J_USER=neo4j
NEO4J_PASSWORD=proxyshop_dev_pw
# No database component on purpose (D39): the logical DB index is the worker index and
# proxyshop_support.redis_client sets it authoritatively. A `/1` here used to be
# silently authoritative instead, landing every worker on DB 1 to FLUSHDB each other.
REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379
SHOPIFY_STUB_URL=http://localhost:8787
EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=hash
LLM_PROVIDER=double
BUYER_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-5
STORE_AGENT_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-5
# The hosted store agent advocates for exactly ONE store and the `BidRequest` on the wire does
# not name one, so the store context is deployment configuration. Both are empty by default and
# an empty STORE_AGENT_CONTEXT is a decision, not an error: the agent answers every solicitation
# `204 store_context_unconfigured` and `collect_bids` represents it at list price (R10). Point
# the first at a JSON store context INSIDE the container (see packages/store-agent/compose.yaml
# for the volume that puts it there); set the second when that file states no domain of its own
# — without a domain the agent emits no `checkout_url` and the exchange's C10 check drops the
# bid from every shortlist.
STORE_AGENT_CONTEXT=
STORE_AGENT_STORE_DOMAIN=
INTERVIEW_MODEL=claude-opus-4-1
EXTRACT_MODEL=claude-haiku-4-5
CHECKOUT_MODE=redirect
SEED_CATEGORY=coffee
RANKING_WEIGHTS=w_m=0.35,w_e=0.20,w_t=0.20,w_v=0.15,w_d=0.10

# The bearer token the merchant service's administrative routes require — discount-code
# minting (`POST /codes`), the envelope routes and the kill switch. It appeared in NO compose
# fragment and NO .env.example at all, so those routes were unreachable in every deployment:
# apps/merchant/svc/src/install/routes.py:63 reads it with no default and no fallback, on
# purpose, so an unset value makes the route refuse every caller with
# `503 {"error":"admin-api-not-configured"}` rather than opening itself.
#
# This is a DEV value and it is not a secret. It is spelled here — unlike the Postgres role
# passwords, which are deliberately absent from this file — because there is nowhere else for
# it to come from: there is no seeded default anywhere in the tree, so a blank here just
# reproduces the 503. Replace it anywhere the merchant API is reachable from outside a
# laptop.
MERCHANT_ADMIN_TOKEN=dev-merchant-admin-token

# Published host ports (D9: every one verified free on this host at scaffold time;
# D40: all env-overridable, and no test may hard-code a port).
PG_PORT=5432
REDIS_PORT=6379
NEO4J_HTTP_PORT=7474
NEO4J_BOLT_PORT=7687

# The service ports. These were documented only in docs/deploy.md ("The new ports are not in
# .env.example — that file belongs to another lane"), which meant the one file an operator
# copies did not mention the ports the stack publishes. Moving the whole block is how a
# second checkout runs its own stack alongside this one (D40).
BUYER_SVC_PORT=8081
MERCHANT_SVC_PORT=8082
EXCHANGE_PORT=8083
TRUST_PORT=8084
INGEST_PORT=8085
STORE_AGENT_PORT=8086
SHOPIFY_STUB_PORT=8787
